{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1385453-how-do-you-prepare-appropiate-opposition-for-the-players","id":1385453,"title":"How do you prepare appropiate opposition for the players?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-03T13:57:24-07:00","id":1385453,"title":"How do you prepare appropiate opposition for the players?","content":"When a pack of werewolfs has to face the idigam and its spirit servitors or a mage cabal confronts a Scelesti with his cult and abbysal intruders, how do you make it a propper boss figth that they have a chance to win instead of something easy or certain death?<br /> <br /> What do you look for when you want to make an encounter where they can: 1-play around and flex their powers, 2-an interesting but winable figth, 3- a challange, 4-a boss figth?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1351-sakii","name":"Sakii","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1351_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-03T15:43:31-07:00","id":1385472,"title":"","content":"The best advice I can think off is to look at what the strengths &amp; weaknesses of the PCs are, &amp; use that to plan strengths &amp; weaknesses of whatever foes you send against them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26055-krat05","name":"Krat05","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"532"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-04T10:20:13-07:00","id":1385618,"title":"","content":"Yeah basically an extension of what Krat05 says.<br /> <br /> You can see the combat capabilities of the PC party through their character sheet. Who has combat skills, who has good Defense, who has little, their health. From there it's a matter of countering that (at different levels of difficulty depending on the situation). You also have to factor in action economy, as even with a group that has just Strength to rely on and no combat skills, having 6 PCs attack a single enemy and they're going to get lucky, especially with how Defense drops for each use of it in a round. This is why so many antagonistic splats lend themselves to being social, having minions in the form of spirits or other monsters.<br /> <br /> Mind you that there's something to be said for keeping an eye on the other side as well. If a monster has a dicepool of 5 but also +5L to its attacks, with even one attack it's going to get close to taking out an average human PC (if your average PC has 7 Health). Since taking the PCs out of battle is harsh on the actual gameplay, I tend to treat antagonists with a 'If they -can- do that much damage, they will' mindset. So if I have a single powerful monster that can take out a PC in one shot, he's likely alone in a situation where the PCs have control, and if he's with minions there's something occupying him so that he can't just wade among them and singleshot the PCs (maybe she has to monitor the computer network, or channel arcane energies into the MacGuffin). There's other ways you can do it, of course, as well as just ignoring that fact (antagonists should have teeth, after all) but balancing out the potential player kill with good timing goes a long way.<br /> <br /> An easy way of sidestepping the unwanted player kill would be to have antagonists that don't necessarily want to kill the players. Maybe a vampire needs the PC so they can control whatever the PCs assets are, or a werewolf needs them because they're the unique kind of individual who can act as an idigam's ban. This kind of situation can justify punches being pulled, or a character whose boxes are full of Xs to survive for the next session. But it can also lead to 'I wish I was dead' moments, where a PC becomes a ghoul or vampire, or a werewolf a slave to an idigam. Would suggest getting input on the player before this to see if they're okay with this kind of result, and especially checking your game to see if it can handle (or you want it to handle) a 'we're monsters now' type change.<br /> <br /> So far these are easy fixes. Balancing antagonist powers to PC capabilities, death isn't the end, minions and meat shields. The hardest part comes when you have a PC who is very combat specialized. There it all comes down to dice and timing. Checking out their capabilities will go a long way here, as you might have abilities that can counter their Fighting Style, or a Fighting Style that can counter their special abilities. But odds are these players are looking for a challenge, and you might be doing them a disservice by not giving them one.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Right there's also situational issues that can help keep a fight from getting out of control, as they are more in the hands of the ST and not the dice. It takes more setup, but having combat scenes take place in, for lack of a better term, arenas where natural changes can drastically change the course of battle can help too. Hurt Locker offers a lot of suggestions for this, mechanically, like fighting on the roof of a train. For years I've been following the BTAS writer's bible which suggested each episode's climactic battle take place in a great showpiece for the city of Gotham, leading to things like battles in clocktowers with massive, shifting gears to, well, fighting on the roof of a train.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 05-04-2020, 01:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-04T19:45:30-07:00","id":1385716,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sakii</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1385453-how-do-you-prepare-appropiate-opposition-for-the-players#post1385453\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When a pack of werewolfs has to face the idigam and its spirit servitors or a mage cabal confronts a Scelesti with his cult and abbysal intruders, how do you make it a propper boss figth that they have a chance to win instead of something easy or certain death?<br /> <br /> What do you look for when you want to make an encounter where they can: 1-play around and flex their powers, 2-an interesting but winable figth, 3- a challange, 4-a boss figth?</div> </div> </div> </div>This question seems to be framed from a DnD mindset of what CR levels a party of X members at level Y can defeat. You can do that in CoD, and that's often fun, but there are other ways to approach conflict. Remember that you can defeat Superman with a spool of piano wire: just wrap Lois Lane to him so that if he flexes his muscles he cuts her in pieces.<br /> <br /> &quot;A challenge&quot; might mean that the player can win, but at some cost. Maybe they turn their friend into a Stigmatic, or lose face to the local Prince, or also incinerate the orphanage. Don't simply threaten their health bar - threaten their Aspirations, Touchstones, Cover. A challenging opponent <i>may decide to run away, </i>because they know enough now to come back with the right weapons. <br /> <br /> A climactic fight should involve drama - betrayals, hidden powers, the reveal of secret agendas and stakes that go beyond life and death. (IMO).<br /> <br /> In a DtD campaign, you might start with a cryptic or maybe a few cult members. This leads to Infrastructure and some hidden plot. The hidden plot leads to more powerful antagonists that respond with the Infrastructure is attacked. Their response might be low level mooks, while simultaneously going for intel on the PC's. The next level of antagonists know something about the PC's and their loved ones or values. Rinse and repeat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24502-kieranmullen","name":"KieranMullen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}